  "worked_solution": "The net of our pyramid is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930500/assets/09eff98ca31b4bb350e2d793dcfa6e6972de25bb3b613c898533068c36fe9a78.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the base of the pyramid is a square with a side equal to $2\\,\\text{m}.$ Hence:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the square}=2\\times 2=4\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEach side face of our solid is triangle, where the base is $2\\,\\text{m}$ and the height is $9\\,\\text{m}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the triangle}=\\frac{1}{2}\\times 2\\times 9=9\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the pyramid consists of $1$ square (base) and $4$ triangles (side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =(\\text{Area of the square})+4\\times (\\text{Area of the triangle}) \\\\  & =4+4\\times 9 \\\\  & =4+36 \\\\  & =40\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
